The tables above: `PLANE` maps lossless distance codes 1 to 120 (entry code minus 1) to a plane offset; `COEF0` and `COEFU` are the VP8 default and update probabilities indexed by type (4), band (8), context (3) and probability (11) in that nesting; `BMODEP` the 4 by 4 mode probabilities indexed by the mode above (10), the mode to the left (10) and the probability (9); `DCTAB` and `ACTAB` the VP8 quantizer steps; `PRED4` describes 4 by 4 intra prediction modes 2 to 9 (16 entries per mode, mode 2 first, pixel x plus 4 y within the mode): an entry's kind is the entry divided by 4096 (1 copy neighbor a, 2 the rounded average of a and b, 3 the rounded three-tap average of a, b and c with b counted twice: a plus twice b plus c plus 2, shifted right by 2), a is the next 4 bits down (entry shifted right by 8, low 4 bits), b the 4 bits below that and c the lowest 4 bits; neighbors 0 to 3 are the pixels left of rows 0 to 3, 4 the pixel above and left, 5 to 12 the 8 pixels above starting at the block's own column. `CATP` holds the extra-bit probabilities of large coefficient categories 3, 4, 5 and 6, which start at entries 0, 3, 7 and 12 and are 3, 4, 5 and 11 long. `CLORDER` is the order of the lossless code length code lengths.

Errors: a module text `why` holds the reason of the last failure; `fail(reason)` stores it and gives false; `error()` gives it. Module `src` is a byte list holding a copy of the file being decoded. `le16(at)`, `le24(at)` and `le32(at)` read little-endian unsigned numbers from src; callers check the bounds first.

Container (`parse()` gives a bool, filling module ints `img_at`, `img_len`, `alph_at`, `alph_len`, `width`, `height`, and module bools `lossless` and `has_alpha`): the file must be at least 20 bytes, start "RIFF" and have "WEBP" at byte 8. The RIFF size at byte 4 must be at least 12, at most 0xfffffff6, and at most the file length minus 8. alph_at is -1 and alph_len 0 until an ALPH chunk is found. The chunk at byte 12 needs its 8-byte header inside the file. When it is "VP8X": its size field must be 10 and the file must hold 30 bytes; flags are the byte at 20, the canvas width is 1 plus `le24(24)` and the height 1 plus `le24(27)`; a canvas whose width times height is 2 to the 32 or more fails; the animation flag (flags bitwise-and 2) fails with "animated WebP is not supported". Then chunks are walked from byte 30, with a running total starting at 22: each needs its 8-byte header inside the file, its size field must not be above 0xfffffff6, its padded size is 8 plus the size rounded up to even, and the total plus the padded size must not be above the RIFF size; the walk stops at a "VP8 " or "VP8L" chunk; any other chunk must fit inside the file, an "ALPH" chunk sets alph_at to its data start and alph_len to its size, and the walk moves past the padded size. Without VP8X the chunk at byte 12 must itself be "VP8 " or "VP8L". The image chunk's size must not be above the RIFF size minus 12 nor above the bytes left after its header; img_at is its data start and img_len its size; lossless is true for "VP8L". Lossy: img_len at least 10, bytes 3, 4, 5 of the data are 0x9d, 0x01, 0x2a; the 24-bit little-endian frame tag at the data start must have bit 0 clear (key frame), the profile (tag shifted right by 1, low 3 bits) at most 3, bit 4 set (shown), and the first partition length (tag shifted right by 5) below img_len; the width is `le16` at data plus 6 bitwise-and 0x3fff and the height `le16` at data plus 8 bitwise-and 0x3fff, both nonzero; has_alpha is whether an ALPH chunk was found. Lossless: img_len at least 5, the first byte 0x2f, the top 3 bits of the byte at data plus 4 zero; the width is 1 plus the low 14 bits of `le32` at data plus 1, the height 1 plus the next 14 bits, has_alpha the next bit. With VP8X the image size must equal the canvas size. A picture of more than 67108864 pixels fails with "picture too big".

Lossless bit reader (module ints `lb_pos` next byte of src to load, `lb_end` end byte, `lb_val` unread bits lowest first, `lb_n` their count, module bool `lb_over`). `lbegin(at, end)` sets them to start reading at at. `lfill()`: while lb_n is at most 48 and bytes are left, add the next byte shifted left by lb_n to lb_val and count 8 more bits. `lpeek(n)` for n from 0 to 24: fill when fewer than n bits are held, then give the low n bits of lb_val (missing bits read as zero). `lskip(n)`: fill when fewer than n bits are held; when still fewer, set lb_over and empty lb_val and lb_n; otherwise drop the low n bits. `lbits(n)` peeks then skips n bits.

Huffman tables live in one module int list `ht`; a table is named by the index of its first entry. `hbuild(lens, n)` takes an int list of n code lengths (one per symbol) and gives a table index, or -1 when the lengths are not a usable code: any length above 15 fails, all lengths zero fails. When exactly one symbol has a nonzero length, the table is 256 entries all holding that symbol with length 0 (it decodes reading no bits). Otherwise the code must be complete: starting from 1, for each length 1 to 15 double the count of open slots and take away the number of codes of that length; going below zero fails, and ending above zero fails. Codes are canonical: symbols sorted by length and then by symbol number get consecutive codes, shorter lengths first; the stream sends each code from its top bit, so the tables are indexed by the bit-reversed code. An entry is length times 65536 plus value. The root has 256 entries: a code of length l up to 8 with reversed code r fills every root index i below 256 whose low l bits equal r, with value the symbol. Codes longer than 8 share root slots by their reversed low 8 bits p; for each such p (in increasing order) a second-level table of 2 to the s entries follows the root and the earlier second-level tables, s being the longest such code's length minus 8, and the root entry p holds length s plus 8 and value the second-level table's offset from the root start; a code of length l in it fills every index j below 2 to the s whose low l minus 8 bits equal its reversed code shifted right by 8, with length l minus 8 and value the symbol. `hread(t)`: take the root entry at the next 8 peeked bits; when its length is at most 8, skip that many bits and give its value; otherwise skip 8 bits, take the entry at t plus its value plus the next (length minus 8) peeked bits, skip that entry's length and give its value.

Reading one lossless code (`lcode(size)` gives a table index or -1; it uses a module int list of size code lengths, all zero first). A first bit 1 is a simple code: the symbol count is 1 plus one bit; the first symbol is 1 bit long when the next bit is 0 and 8 bits long otherwise, the second symbol (when there are two) is 8 bits; each named symbol below size gets length 1 (symbols at or above size are ignored). Otherwise: the count of code length code lengths is 4 plus 4 bits, read as 3-bit numbers into a 19-entry list at the positions given by `CLORDER` (the rest 0), built with `hbuild` (failing when it fails). Then when a bit is 1, a limit: its bit width is 2 plus twice a 3-bit number and the limit is 2 plus a number of that width, failing when it is above size; otherwise the limit is size. Symbols are filled from 0 while fewer than size are filled and the limit (counted down once per code read) is not used up: a code below 16 is that symbol's length and, when nonzero, becomes the previous length (which starts at 8); 16 repeats the previous length 3 plus a 2-bit number of times; 17 writes zeros 3 plus a 3-bit number of times; 18 writes zeros 11 plus a 7-bit number of times; a repeat going past size fails. Running past the end of the data (lb_over) fails. Finally the lengths are built with `hbuild`.

Lossless image stream (`stream(w, h, top, out)` gives a bool and replaces the int list out with w times h pixels, each 32-bit ARGB as alpha, red, green, blue from the top byte down). The decoding width starts at w. When top is true the module transform list is emptied and, while a bit is 1, a transform is read: its type is 2 bits and each type may appear once (else fail). Types 0 (predictor) and 1 (cross color): bits is 2 plus 3 bits, then its data is `stream` of the decoding width and h each divided by 2 to the bits rounded up, not top. Type 2 (subtract green) has no data. Type 3 (color indexing): the color count is 1 plus 8 bits; bits is 0 above 16 colors, 1 above 4, 2 above 2, else 3; its data is `stream` of the color count by 1, not top, then expanded: each byte of each color after the first is added to the same byte of the color before it, modulo 256, and the list grows with zero colors to 2 to the (8 shifted right by bits) entries; the decoding width becomes itself divided by 2 to the bits rounded up. Every transform records its type, bits, the decoding width it was read at (before color indexing narrows it) and its data. Then when a bit is 1, the color cache bits are 4 bits, which must be from 1 to 11. Then when top is true and a bit is 1, meta codes: the meta bits are 2 plus 3 bits and the entropy image is `stream` of the decoding width and h each divided by 2 to the meta bits rounded up, not top; the group count is the largest of (pixel shifted right by 8, low 16 bits) plus 1. Otherwise there is one group. Each group then reads 5 codes with `lcode`: green of size 280 plus the cache size (2 to the cache bits, 0 without a cache), red, blue and alpha of size 256 and distance of size 40. The cache size, meta bits, entropy image and group table indexes are locals of this call, because nested streams run while they are being read. Then the pixels (the decoding width by h) are decoded, and when top is true the inverse transforms are applied (out ends w by h).

Lossless pixels. Positions go from 0 to the pixel count with a column x and row y. The group used for a symbol is the one at the current position: with meta codes, the entropy pixel at (y shifted right by meta bits) times the entropy width plus (x shifted right by meta bits), shifted right by 8, low 16 bits (it only changes when x crosses a multiple of 2 to the meta bits, at a new row or after a copy). A green symbol below 256 is a literal: red, blue and alpha symbols follow and the pixel is alpha times 2 to the 24 plus red times 65536 plus green times 256 plus blue. Below 280 it is a copy: its length is `copylen(symbol minus 256)`, then a distance symbol gives the distance code `copylen(distance symbol)`; codes above 120 mean a distance of the code minus 120, otherwise with the `PLANE` entry e for the code, the distance is (e shifted right by 4) times the decoding width plus 8 minus (e bitwise-and 15), at least 1; a distance above the position or a length above the pixels left fails; the copy goes one pixel at a time from the distance back, so it may overlap itself. `copylen(s)` is s plus 1 for s below 4, else with extra bits e equal to (s minus 2) shifted right by 1, it is ((2 plus (s bitwise-and 1)) shifted left by e) plus an e-bit number plus 1. Below 280 plus the cache size it takes cache entry (symbol minus 280). Anything else fails. Every pixel written (literal, copied or from the cache) is put into the cache in order: its slot is (0x1e35a7bd times the pixel, low 32 bits) shifted right by 32 minus the cache bits. Reading past the end (lb_over) fails, checked at every row end and at the end.

Inverse transforms, from the last read to the first, each on an image whose width is the transform's recorded width (color indexing turns its narrow input into that width). Pixel arithmetic is per byte (alpha, red, green, blue). Subtract green: red and blue each get green added, modulo 256. Cross color: each pixel uses the transform data pixel at (y shifted right by bits) times (width divided by 2 to the bits rounded up) plus (x shifted right by bits), whose low byte is green-to-red, next byte green-to-blue, next red-to-blue, each read as a signed byte; with green read as a signed byte, red becomes red plus (green-to-red times green, arithmetic shift right by 5), modulo 256, and blue becomes blue plus (green-to-blue times green, arithmetic shift right by 5) plus (red-to-blue times the new red read as a signed byte, arithmetic shift right by 5), modulo 256. Predictor, in place in raster order, each stored pixel being the residual added per byte modulo 256 to a prediction from already finished pixels (L left, T above, TL above left, TR above right, where TR of the last column is the first pixel of the current row): the top-left pixel predicts 0xff000000, the rest of row 0 predicts L, column 0 of later rows predicts T, others use the mode (data pixel at the tile as for cross color, shifted right by 8, low 4 bits): 0 0xff000000, 1 L, 2 T, 3 TR, 4 TL, 5 avg(avg(L, TR), T), 6 avg(L, TL), 7 avg(L, T), 8 avg(TL, T), 9 avg(T, TR), 10 avg(avg(L, TL), avg(T, TR)), 11 select, 12 per byte L plus T minus TL clamped to 0 to 255, 13 per byte with a the byte of avg(L, T): a plus the C division of (a minus TL) by 2, clamped to 0 to 255, 14 and 15 like 0; avg is the per-byte average rounded down; select sums over the 4 bytes the distance of L from TL minus the distance of T from TL, and predicts T when the sum is at most 0, else L. Color indexing: with bits 0 each output pixel is color (pixel shifted right by 8, low 8 bits); otherwise each input pixel's green byte packs 2 to the bits indexes of 8 shifted right by bits bits each, lowest first, giving the outputs left to right, each row starting a new input pixel.

Lossless picture (`vp8l(px)`): bit reading starts after the 5-byte header, `stream(width, height, true, ...)`, and each ARGB pixel is written as red, green, blue, alpha bytes.

Lossy bool decoders: 9 at once (0 the first partition, 1 to 8 the token partitions), kept in parallel module int lists: next byte, end byte, value, range (stored minus 1), bit count and an end flag. `binit(r, at, size)`: range 254, value 0, bit count -8, flag clear, then one load. Load: when a byte is left, the bit count grows by 8 and the value becomes itself shifted left by 8 plus the byte; else, when the flag is clear, the value is shifted left by 8, the bit count grows by 8 and the flag is set; else the bit count becomes 0. `bit(r, p)` reads one bit with probability p of zero (p from 0 to 255): load first when the bit count is below 0; split is (range times p) shifted right by 8; the bit is 1 when the value shifted right by the bit count is above split, and then range becomes range minus split and the value loses (split plus 1) shifted left by the bit count, else range becomes split plus 1; then with shift equal to 7 minus the position of range's highest set bit, range is shifted left by shift, the bit count drops by shift and the stored range is the new range minus 1. `bsign(r, v)`: load when the bit count is below 0; split is the stored range shifted right by 1; when the value shifted right by the bit count is above split, the stored range becomes (stored range minus 1) bitwise-or 1, the value loses (split plus 1) shifted left by the bit count and the result is minus v; otherwise the stored range becomes itself bitwise-or 1 and the result is v; either way the bit count drops by 1. `bval(r, n)` reads n bits with probability 128, highest first. `bsval(r, n)` reads `bval(r, n)` then one bit with probability 128 that, when 1, negates it.

Lossy headers (`vp8_head()` gives a bool). From img_at: the 3-byte tag and 7 bytes of key frame header are already checked; the picture is width by height, mb_w and mb_h are (width plus 15) shifted right by 4 and the same for height. The first partition (length tag shifted right by 5) starts at img_at plus 10 and must fit in the chunk; when mb_w times mb_h is above 8 times the first partition length plus 256 it fails with "damaged frame" (real files always fit). Decoder 0 reads it: colorspace bit and clamp bit (both ignored). Segment header: when a bit is 1 segments are used: update map is a bit; when a bit is 1, absolute deltas is a bit and then 4 quantizer values and 4 filter strengths follow, each a bit that when 1 gives `bsval` of 7 bits (quantizers) or 6 bits (strengths), else 0; with update map 3 segment probabilities follow, each a bit that when 1 gives `bval(8)`, else 255. Without segments the map is not updated; absolute deltas default to true, quantizers and strengths to 0, probabilities to 255. Filter header: simple bit, level `bval(6)`, sharpness `bval(3)`; when a bit is 1 deltas are used and when another bit is 1 they are updated: 4 reference deltas then 4 mode deltas, each a bit that when 1 gives `bsval(6)` (all 8 start at 0). The filter type is 0 when the level is 0, else 1 for simple and 2 otherwise. Fail when decoder 0's end flag is set. Partitions: the count is 2 to the `bval(2)`; after the first partition come 3-byte little-endian sizes for all but the last (fail when they do not fit), then the partitions in order, each size cut down to what is left, the last taking the rest, which must not be empty. Quantizers: base `bval(7)`, then 5 deltas (y1 dc, y2 dc, y2 ac, uv dc, uv ac) each a bit that when 1 gives `bsval(4)`, else 0. For each of 4 segments q is the segment quantizer (plus base unless absolute deltas) when segments are used, else base; with clamp(v, m) giving v limited to 0 to m: y1 dc is `DCTAB` of clamp(q plus y1 dc delta, 127), y1 ac `ACTAB` of clamp(q, 127), y2 dc twice `DCTAB` of clamp(q plus y2 dc delta, 127), y2 ac (`ACTAB` of clamp(q plus y2 ac delta, 127) times 101581) shifted right by 16 but at least 8, uv dc `DCTAB` of clamp(q plus uv dc delta, 117), uv ac `ACTAB` of clamp(q plus uv ac delta, 127). Then one bit is read and ignored, then the 1056 coefficient probabilities in table order: each is `bval(8)` when a bit with the `COEFU` probability is 1, else the `COEF0` value; then a skip bit: when 1 the skip probability is `bval(8)` and skipping is used.

Lossy macroblock modes for one row (decoder 0; module lists: per macroblock of the row its segment, skip, i4 flag, 16 sub-block modes, chroma mode; across the picture 4 top modes per macroblock column, starting 0; 4 left modes reset to 0 at each row). Segment: with update map, a bit with segment probability 0 picks between a bit with probability 1 (when 0) and 2 plus a bit with probability 2 (when 1); else 0. Skip: a bit with the skip probability when skipping is used, else 0. The macroblock is i4 when a bit with probability 145 is 0. Otherwise its mode is: when a bit with 156 is 1, then a bit with 128 picks 1 (TM) when 1 or 3 (H) when 0; when it is 0, a bit with 163 picks 2 (V) when 1 or 0 (DC) when 0; the 4 top and 4 left modes all become it. i4: for each sub-block row y and column x, with probabilities the 9 `BMODEP` entries for the top mode of column x and the left mode of row y, the mode comes from the tree: bit 0 zero gives 0; else bit 1 zero gives 1; else bit 2 zero gives 2; else when bit 3 is zero, bit 4 zero gives 3, else bit 5 picks 4 (zero) or 5; when bit 3 is one, bit 6 zero gives 6, else bit 7 zero gives 7, else bit 8 picks 8 (zero) or 9; the mode becomes column x's top mode, row y's left mode and sub-block x plus 4 y's mode. Chroma mode: a bit with 142 zero gives 0, else a bit with 114 zero gives 2, else a bit with 183 picks 1 (one) or 3. Fail when decoder 0's end flag is set after the row.

Lossy coefficients (`coeffs(r, type, ctx, dq_dc, dq_ac, first, at)` gives the count n as in the reference and writes into the module int list `co` at at). The probability set for position n is band `BANDS[n]` of the type. p starts as context ctx of position first's band. For n from first while n is below 16: when a bit with p's probability 0 is 0, give n; then while a bit with probability 1 is 0, n goes up and p becomes context 0 of the new n's band, giving 16 when n reaches 16; then when a bit with probability 2 is 0 the value is 1 and the next p is context 1 of n plus 1's band, else the value is `large(p)` and the next p is context 2 of n plus 1's band; `co` at at plus `ZIGZAG[n]` gets `bsign` of the value times dq_dc for n 0 or dq_ac otherwise, kept as a signed 16-bit number (wrapping); n goes up. Give 16 after the loop. `large(p)`: when a bit with p[3] is 0, the value is 2 when a bit with p[4] is 0, else 3 plus a bit with p[5]; when it is 1 and a bit with p[6] is 0: 5 plus a bit with 159 when a bit with p[7] is 0, else 7 plus twice a bit with 165 plus a bit with 145; otherwise b1 is a bit with p[8], b0 a bit with p[9 plus b1], category 2 times b1 plus b0, v reads one bit per `CATP` probability of that category doubling v each time and adding the bit, and the value is v plus 3 plus (8 shifted left by the category).

Lossy residuals for a macroblock (module ints per macroblock column: top nz bits and top dc nz; left ones reset to 0 at each row; 384 coefficients per macroblock of the row, zero first, 16 per sub-block: 16 luma, 4 u, 4 v). Unless the macroblock is skipped (skip flag set while skipping is used), with the segment's quantizers: when not i4, the 16 dc values come from `coeffs` of type 1 with context top dc nz plus left dc nz, y2 quantizers and first 0 into a scratch block; both dc nz become whether that count is above 0; when the count is above 1 the inverse Walsh-Hadamard transform writes the 16 luma sub-blocks' coefficient 0, else every luma sub-block's coefficient 0 is (dc 0 plus 3) arithmetic shifted right by 3; luma then uses type 0 starting at 1, else type 3 starting at 0. Walsh-Hadamard: for i 0 to 3, a0 is in[i] plus in[12 plus i], a1 in[4 plus i] plus in[8 plus i], a2 in[4 plus i] minus in[8 plus i], a3 in[i] minus in[12 plus i]; tmp[i] is a0 plus a1, tmp[8 plus i] a0 minus a1, tmp[4 plus i] a3 plus a2, tmp[12 plus i] a3 minus a2; then for i 0 to 3 with dc tmp[4 i] plus 3, a0 dc plus tmp[4 i plus 3], a1 tmp[4 i plus 1] plus tmp[4 i plus 2], a2 tmp[4 i plus 1] minus tmp[4 i plus 2], a3 dc minus tmp[4 i plus 3], sub-blocks 4 i, 4 i plus 1, 4 i plus 2 and 4 i plus 3 get coefficient 0 of (a0 plus a1), (a3 plus a2), (a0 minus a1) and (a3 minus a2), each arithmetic shifted right by 3. Luma sub-blocks in raster order: the context is the left flag of the sub-block row plus the top flag of the sub-block column; after `coeffs` with y1 quantizers both flags become whether the count is above first; each sub-block gets a 2-bit code: 3 when the count is above 3, 2 when above 1, else 1 when its coefficient 0 is nonzero, else 0. Then u and v (type 2, first 0, uv quantizers) as 2 by 2 sub-blocks each with their own 2 top and 2 left flags per plane, coded the same way. The top and left nz bits keep 4 luma flags (bits 0 to 3), then 2 u flags (bits 4, 5) and 2 v flags (bits 6, 7). The macroblock has coefficients when any code is nonzero. A skipped macroblock gets all codes 0 and zeroes the top and left nz bits, and both dc nz too when it is not i4. Fail when the token decoder's end flag is set after the macroblock.

Lossy reconstruction of a row into a work area of 832 bytes with stride 32: luma at 40, u at 584, v at 600, each read through positions relative to a block start (minus 1 is the left pixel, minus 32 the pixel above). At each row start the 16 luma and 8 u and 8 v pixels left of each block are 129; on row 0 the row above luma from 1 left to 20 right of the start is 127 and the row above chroma from 1 left to 8 right is 127, on later rows only the above-left corners are 129. Per macroblock: after the first, the 4 pixels left of each of rows minus 1 to 15 (luma) and minus 1 to 7 (u, v) are copied from that row's last 4 pixels (columns 12 to 15 luma, 4 to 7 chroma); on rows after 0 the row above comes from saved top lines (16 luma, 8 u, 8 v per macroblock column). i4: the 4 pixels right of the row above (columns 16 to 19) come, on rows after 0, from the next column's saved top luma, or 4 copies of this column's saved pixel 15 for the last column; they are also copied to columns 16 to 19 of luma rows 3, 7 and 11; then each sub-block n (at column 4 times (n bitwise-and 3), row 4 times (n shifted right by 2)) is predicted with its mode and its transform added. Not i4: predicted as a whole with the 16 by 16 mode, then the transforms added. Chroma: both planes predicted with the chroma mode, then their transforms. On every row but the last, the saved top lines get luma row 15 and chroma row 7. The block is copied out to the full luma and chroma planes (mb_w times 16 by mb_h times 16 and half that).

Lossy prediction. 16 by 16 and 8 by 8 (size s): mode 0 (DC) averages the s pixels above and the s left (sum plus s, shifted right by log2 of 2 s), or only those that exist with half the rounding and one less shift (no row above on row 0, no left on column 0), or fills 128 when neither exists; 1 (TM) is left of the row plus the pixel above minus the above-left pixel, clamped to 0 to 255; 2 (V) copies the row above; 3 (H) copies each row's left pixel. 4 by 4: mode 0 fills (sum of the 4 above and 4 left plus 4) shifted right by 3; mode 1 is TM as above; modes 2 to 9 follow `PRED4`. These all read the work area as is, borders included.

Lossy inverse transforms on a 4 by 4 block of coefficients in (index x plus 4 y) added to the predicted pixels, each result clamped to 0 to 255. Each sub-block's 2-bit code picks: 3 the full transform, 2 the full transform too (only coefficients 0, 1, 4 can be nonzero, same result), 1 the dc only transform, 0 nothing. With m1(a) equal to ((a times 20091) arithmetic shifted right by 16) plus a and m2(a) equal to (a times 35468) arithmetic shifted right by 16: first pass for i 0 to 3: a is in[i] plus in[8 plus i], b in[i] minus in[8 plus i], c m2(in[4 plus i]) minus m1(in[12 plus i]), d m1(in[4 plus i]) plus m2(in[12 plus i]); tmp[4 i] a plus d, tmp[4 i plus 1] b plus c, tmp[4 i plus 2] b minus c, tmp[4 i plus 3] a minus d; second pass for output row i: dc is tmp[i] plus 4, a dc plus tmp[8 plus i], b dc minus tmp[8 plus i], c m2(tmp[4 plus i]) minus m1(tmp[12 plus i]), d m1(tmp[4 plus i]) plus m2(tmp[12 plus i]); the 4 pixels of row i get (a plus d), (b plus c), (b minus c), (a minus d) each arithmetic shifted right by 3 added. Dc only adds (in[0] plus 4) arithmetic shifted right by 3 to all 16. Chroma: when any of a plane's 4 codes is nonzero, if any code is 2 or 3 all 4 sub-blocks get the full transform, else each sub-block whose coefficient 0 is nonzero gets the dc only one.

Lossy loop filter, after the whole picture is reconstructed, over the full planes macroblock by macroblock in raster order (it never feeds prediction). Strengths per segment and i4 flag: the level is the segment strength (plus the filter level unless absolute deltas) when segments are used, else the filter level; with deltas used add reference delta 0 and, for i4, mode delta 0; clamp to 0 to 63. A level 0 means no filtering for that kind; else ilevel starts at the level, is shifted right by 2 for sharpness above 4 or by 1 for sharpness 1 to 4, is limited to 9 minus sharpness when sharpness is above 0, and is at least 1; limit is twice the level plus ilevel; hev threshold 2 at level 40 or more, 1 at 15 or more, else 0. Inner edges are filtered when the macroblock is i4 or has coefficients. Simple filter (luma only): left edge when not column 0 with limit plus 4, inner vertical edges at columns 4, 8, 12 with limit, top edge when not row 0 with limit plus 4, inner horizontal edges at rows 4, 8, 12 with limit, each across all 16 lines: a line is filtered when 4 times |p0 minus q0| plus |p1 minus q1| is at most twice the threshold plus 1, by the 2-tap change. Normal filter in the same order (left edge, inner vertical, top edge, inner horizontal), luma 16 lines and each chroma plane 8 lines (chroma inner edges only at 4): a line needs 4 times |p0 minus q0| plus |p1 minus q1| at most twice the threshold plus 1 and |p3 minus p2|, |p2 minus p1|, |p1 minus p0|, |q3 minus q2|, |q2 minus q1|, |q1 minus q0| all at most ilevel; then when |p1 minus p0| or |q1 minus q0| is above the hev threshold it gets the 2-tap change, otherwise macroblock edges get the 6-tap change and inner edges the 4-tap change. With s1 clamping to minus 128 to 127, s2 clamping to minus 16 to 15 and c clamping to 0 to 255: 2-tap: a is 3 times (q0 minus p0) plus s1(p1 minus q1), p0 becomes c(p0 plus s2((a plus 3) arithmetic shifted right by 3)), q0 becomes c(q0 minus s2((a plus 4) arithmetic shifted right by 3)). 4-tap: a is 3 times (q0 minus p0), a1 s2((a plus 4) shifted), a2 s2((a plus 3) shifted), a3 (a1 plus 1) arithmetic shifted right by 1; p1 plus a3, p0 plus a2, q0 minus a1, q1 minus a3, each through c. 6-tap: a is s1(3 times (q0 minus p0) plus s1(p1 minus q1)), a1 (27 a plus 63), a2 (18 a plus 63), a3 (9 a plus 63) each arithmetic shifted right by 7; p2 plus a3, p1 plus a2, p0 plus a1, q0 minus a1, q1 minus a2, q2 minus a3, each through c.

Lossy to RGBA with fancy upsampling. For luma row r the near chroma row is r shifted right by 1 and the far one is (r plus 1) shifted right by 1 for odd r and (r shifted right by 1) minus 1 for even r, both limited to the chroma rows that exist ((height plus 1) shifted right by 1 of them). For u and v separately, with N and F the near and far rows: pixel 0 gets (3 N[0] plus F[0] plus 2) shifted right by 2; for i from 1 to (width minus 1) shifted right by 1, with a N[i minus 1], b N[i], c F[i minus 1], d F[i] and S a plus b plus c plus d plus 8, pixel 2 i minus 1 gets (((S plus 2 (b plus c)) shifted right by 3) plus a) shifted right by 1 and pixel 2 i gets (((S plus 2 (a plus d)) shifted right by 3) plus b) shifted right by 1; for an even width the last pixel gets (3 N[last] plus F[last] plus 2) shifted right by 2 with last the final chroma column. With mh(v, k) equal to (v times k) shifted right by 8 and clip8(v) giving v shifted right by 6 when v is from 0 to 16383, 0 below that and 255 above: red is clip8(mh(y, 19077) plus mh(v, 26149) minus 14234), green clip8(mh(y, 19077) minus mh(u, 6419) minus mh(v, 13320) plus 8708), blue clip8(mh(y, 19077) plus mh(u, 33050) minus 17685), alpha 255.

Lossy alpha (`alpha(px)` gives a bool, writing every 4th byte from 3). The ALPH data's first byte: method its low 2 bits, filter the next 2, preprocessing the next 2, the top 2 bits reserved; fail when the data is shorter than 2 bytes, the method is above 1, the preprocessing above 1 or the reserved bits are not 0. Method 0 needs width times height bytes after that byte, taken as the rows. Method 1 reads a lossless `stream(width, height, true, ...)` from the byte after and takes each pixel's green byte. Then the rows are unfiltered in order, each from its own row and the finished row above (none for row 0): filter 0 none; 1 horizontal: each value adds the one before it, the first adding the row above's first value (0 on row 0); 2 vertical: on row 0 like horizontal, else each adds the value above; 3 gradient: on row 0 like horizontal, else with left, top and top-left all starting at the row above's first value, for each column top is the value above, the value adds left plus top minus top-left clamped to 0 to 255 and becomes the new left, and top-left becomes top. All sums are modulo 256.

`vp8(px)` decodes the lossy picture: `vp8_head`, then for each macroblock row the modes, each macroblock's residuals from token partition (row bitwise-and (partition count minus 1)), then the row's reconstruction; then the loop filter when the filter type is above 0; then the RGBA conversion of the width by height picture; then the alpha when has_alpha (failing with "bad alpha" when it fails).

`is_webp(b)` gives whether b is at least 12 bytes with "RIFF" at 0 and "WEBP" at 8. `size(b)` copies b to src and runs `parse`, giving width, height and the result. `decode(data, px)` empties px, copies data to src, parses, and runs `vp8l` or `vp8`; on success px holds width times height times 4 bytes and it gives width, height, true; on failure px is emptied and it gives 0, 0, false.
